# 11. Interview Explanation (30–60 sec)

"I first sort the intervals by starting point in ascending order. If two intervals have the same start, I place the one with the larger end first. Then I scan the sorted list while keeping track of the maximum end seen so far. If the current interval's end is greater than `max_end`, it cannot be covered, so I count it and update `max_end`. Otherwise, it is covered by a previous interval and I ignore it. Sorting takes `O(n log n)` time, and the scan is `O(n)`, so the overall complexity is `O(n log n)`."

---

# 12. Key Takeaways

* Sort by `(start ascending, end descending)`.
* Use a single variable `max_end`.
* If `current_end <= max_end`, the interval is covered.
* Only one linear scan is needed after sorting.
* Overall complexity is `O(n log n)`.

---

# 13. Common Mistakes

* ❌ Sorting by `(start, end)` instead of `(start, -end)`.
* ❌ Updating `max_end` even for covered intervals.
* ❌ Using `<` instead of `<=` when reasoning about coverage.
* ❌ Merging intervals (this problem is **not** interval merging).
* ❌ Forgetting that intervals with the same start must be ordered by decreasing end.

